    <h2>FAQ</h2>

    <h3>Is Monday better than Asana?</h3>
    <p>Monday is better for visual project tracking with colorful boards and flexible layouts. Asana is better for structured task management with hierarchies, dependencies, and goals alignment. For marketing and operations teams that want visual dashboards, Monday wins. For cross-functional teams that need task hierarchies and goals tracking, Asana wins.</p>

    <h3>Which is cheaper, Monday or Asana?</h3>
    <p>Monday is slightly cheaper. Monday Basic starts at $9 per user per month, while Asana Starter starts at $10.99 per user per month. Both offer free tiers for small teams: Monday supports up to 2 users, Asana supports up to 10 users with limited features. For larger teams, Monday's per-user pricing is slightly lower.</p>

    <h3>Can Monday and Asana integrate with each other?</h3>
    <p>Monday and Asana do not integrate natively, but you can connect them through third-party tools like Zapier, Make, or Workato. Common use cases include creating a Monday item when an Asana task is completed, or syncing project status between platforms. Most teams pick one platform to avoid synchronization overhead.</p>

    <h3>Which has better automation?</h3>
    <p>Monday has a more intuitive visual automation builder with recipes like "When status changes to Done, notify team". Asana has more powerful rule-based automation with multi-step rules and custom triggers on Business and Enterprise plans. For simple automations, Monday wins on ease. For complex multi-step workflows, Asana wins on power.</p>

    <h3>Does Asana have a timeline view?</h3>
    <p>Yes, Asana has a native timeline view (Gantt chart) that shows task dependencies and project schedules over time. It supports drag-and-drop rescheduling and dependency tracking. Monday also has a timeline view, but Asana's is more mature for managing complex project schedules with dependencies.</p>

    <h3>Which is better for software development teams?</h3>
    <p>Neither Monday nor Asana is optimized for software development. Both support Kanban boards and integrations with GitHub and Jira, but neither has native sprint planning, burndown charts, or developer-specific workflows. For software development, consider Linear, Jira, or GitHub Projects instead. For cross-functional work that includes engineering, Asana is slightly better due to dependencies.</p>

